# Catch edit-based mass-ping evasion: a member posts something harmless, then
# edits @everyone/@here into it once the message has scrolled away. Only the
# CURRENT text is scanned (the legacy filter did the same) — the dispatcher
# already drops no-op edits, so every fire here is a real content change.
#
# Staff are exempt twice over: anyone holding Manage Messages, and anyone
# carrying the configured staff role. The role id is baked in as a literal at
# install time — see the extension manifest. Nothing is read from Discord or the
# DB, so an ordinary typo fix costs a couple of string scans and nothing else.
STAFF_ROLE_ID = {{cfg.staff_role_id}}

edited_content = context["message_content"]
author_is_staff = (
    context["author_has_manage_messages"]
    or STAFF_ROLE_ID in context["author_role_ids"]
)

if not author_is_staff:
    if "@everyone" in edited_content or "@here" in edited_content:
        # The notice deliberately does NOT quote the offending text back: echoing
        # it would put the very mention the handler is policing into the bot's
        # own message. message_edit is channel-keyed, so both the notice and the
        # delete land in the channel the edit happened in.
        await send_message(
            "<@" + context["author_id"] + "> please don't edit a server-wide "
            + "mention into a message after posting it — that edit has been "
            + "removed. Ask a moderator if you need to reach everyone."
        )
        await delete_message(context["message_id"])
